{
  "suite": "lab.manifest",
  "spec": "0.6.0",
  "description": "Dataset manifests and the digest scope, spec section 10. `input.manifest` is a manifest document (10.1). `input.disk`, when present, maps a path to the exact UTF-8 text of a file written under a scratch root before the manifest is checked against it, so a case can pin a `bytes` that disagrees with the file it names (10.4). `expect.data_hash` is the manifest's `data_hash` (10.2): the content hash (4.2) of the canonical JSON (4.1) of the `{path, sha256, bytes}` array sorted by `path` byte-wise. `expect.error` is the registry code of the refusal (10.4). A case whose `captured_at` is not a string pins 10.4's type clause: 10.1 gives that member as an RFC 3339 `full-date` string, so a number must not be read as a date, because an implementation that coerces one reports a capture that never happened. Every `sha256` in an input is a literal stated by the case, never read back from an implementation.",
  "source": "Spec section 10 and the Wave 2 plan (docs/superpowers/plans/). Derived without this kit's code. Each file's `sha256` is hashlib.sha256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the literal text the case names (a.csv = 'id,value\\n1,2\\n', whose digest `shasum -a 256` reproduces: c15097c4...d93c), and `bytes` is the length of those bytes. Each `expect.data_hash` is 'sha256:' + hashlib.sha256 over rfc8785.dumps (the RFC 8785 reference package, pinned by conformance/hashing/) of the array of {path, sha256, bytes} objects sorted by path.encode('utf-8'), with no concatenation of file bytes. The refusal cases are the seven of 10.4, the unknown and missing top-level members of 10.1, and 10.4's type clause (a `captured_at` that is a number or null); each is invalid by reading the clause, not by running a parser. The numeric cases expect no date at all, which is the point: 0 and 1764028800 are there because a lax JSON-to-date coercion reads them as 1970-01-01 and 2026-11-25, and 10.4 says such a member is invalid rather than either of those dates.",
